<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Formats
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle Title="Display Format" />

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Separate display and input formats make dates easy to read and quick to enter.
    Users can read full month names while entering short numeric dates.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    For example, <Code>dd.MM.yyyy</Code> shows and accepts dates such as <Code>27.07.2026</Code>.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Ordinal days">
        <Paragraph>
            Ordinal day numbers, such as <Code>1st</Code>, <Code>2nd</Code>, and <Code>3rd</Code>, give dates a familiar written form.
            Appointments and bookings can show the full date, such as <Code>Tuesday 3rd November 2026</Code>, with day and month names in the user's selected language.
        </Paragraph>
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="DatePickerOrdinalExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <DatePickerOrdinalExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle Title="Day numbers and languages" />

<DocsPageParagraph>
    In <Code>DisplayFormat</Code>, <Code>{day:ordinal}</Code> shows the day number in the selected language, such as <Code>3rd</Code> in English or <Code>3.</Code> in Croatian.
    It works with date formats such as <Code>dddd</Code> for the weekday and <Code>MMMM</Code> for the month name.
    Ordinal formatting is currently available for the day number only.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Weekday names, month names, and day numbers update when the selected Blazorise language changes.
    Each language has its own way of writing dates. Some add letters to the day number, some add a period, and others use just the number.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<Table>
    <TableHeader>
        <TableRow>
            <TableHeaderCell>Language</TableHeaderCell>
            <TableHeaderCell>Day examples</TableHeaderCell>
        </TableRow>
    </TableHeader>
    <TableBody>
        <TableRow>
            <TableRowCell>English</TableRowCell>
            <TableRowCell><Code>1st</Code>, <Code>2nd</Code>, <Code>3rd</Code>, <Code>11th</Code></TableRowCell>
        </TableRow>
        <TableRow>
            <TableRowCell>French</TableRowCell>
            <TableRowCell><Code>1er</Code>, <Code>2</Code>, <Code>3</Code></TableRowCell>
        </TableRow>
        <TableRow>
            <TableRowCell>Spanish</TableRowCell>
            <TableRowCell><Code>1.º</Code>, <Code>2</Code>, <Code>3</Code></TableRowCell>
        </TableRow>
        <TableRow>
            <TableRowCell>Italian and Portuguese</TableRowCell>
            <TableRowCell><Code>1º</Code>, <Code>2</Code>, <Code>3</Code></TableRowCell>
        </TableRow>
        <TableRow>
            <TableRowCell>Croatian, Czech, Danish, German, Icelandic, and Slovak</TableRowCell>
            <TableRowCell><Code>1.</Code>, <Code>2.</Code>, <Code>3.</Code></TableRowCell>
        </TableRow>
        <TableRow>
            <TableRowCell>Chinese, Dutch, Polish, Romanian, Russian, and Turkish</TableRowCell>
            <TableRowCell><Code>1</Code>, <Code>2</Code>, <Code>3</Code></TableRowCell>
        </TableRow>
    </TableBody>
</Table>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    These day forms are included for all supported DatePicker languages.
    If an ordinal translation is missing, DatePicker shows just the day number.
    For languages such as Croatian and German, <Code>{day:ordinal}</Code> already includes the period after the number.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    <Code>InputFormat</Code> supports numeric date entry, such as <Code>dd.MM.yyyy</Code>, while <Code>{day:ordinal}</Code> is supported only in <Code>DisplayFormat</Code>.
    Without an <Code>InputFormat</Code>, users can also type the date as displayed, including its translated names and day number.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Mobile browsers normally use their own date display.
    The example uses <Code>DisableMobile</Code> so dates keep the same display format on desktop and mobile.
</DocsPageParagraph>

